A||N||16||Seanh||Removeing Cat Converter||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||05-10-2001||09:54 PM||||I still have my damn converter on. I was going to take it off, but I don't want to loose any torque as that is what I am going for. How much torque do you actually loose? Is it very noticeable? How noticable? I am very concerned with loosing any off the line power, but I would like the improved sound and gas milage. Think I should do it? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||LivoniaFieroGT||05-10-2001||10:50 PM||||On both my GT's I've taken the cats off.  The sound on both was way more mellow and after a short burn time the mufflers had a good gurgle on deacceleration.  Noticed no appreciable increase and certainly no decrease in performance.  One the older GT I did it 11 years ago and still running original muffler.  Have gone thru 2 cat bypass pipes... must have been cheaper steel.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||05-11-2001||12:02 AM||||You didn't notice a torque decrease? I know there is one, but I am just wondering how noticable it really is. Iv heard like 7 ftlbs of torque, which I don't think I would really miss. So you didn't notice any gas milage increase either? I would think logically this would result, and Im needing every little drop I squeeze out of that tank with my gas prices and how much I drive all the time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sjp777||05-11-2001||12:06 AM||||This topic has been covered to death.<P>OK, one more time. If you replace the cat with just a straight pipe, the car will be very loud and sounds too tinny.<P>Replace it instead with a 22" straight cherry bomb muffler with 2 1/4" outlets that will fit over the stock pipes and can be clamped down with muffler clamps. The cherry bomb gives just enough back pressure and a very cool throaty sound to the exhaust. You can get the cherry bomb just about anywhere for less than $20<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MAT88GT||05-11-2001||12:08 AM||||I've had mine off for 'bout 3 years now...if you lose any torque it wont be noticable by the seat of the pants. I made a replacement pipe from 16ga aluminized steel which has held up great even with Iowa winters.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Mustang Gt 351w/c4 trans, custom 6pt <BR>1985 Fiero Gt some mods<BR>1986 Fiero 2m4 mint condition||||reg||1
Z||000005||MAT88GT||05-11-2001||12:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Replace it instead with a 22" straight cherry bomb muffler with 2 1/4" outlets that will fit over the stock pipes and can be clamped down with muffler clamps. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldn't put a cherry bomb on my car to save my life. As for the tiny sound, what muffler are you running? My 85 doesn't have any sort of tiny sound, but if you have a flowmaster muffler I could fully understand your point of view.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||05-11-2001||12:11 AM||||60 degree V6s have tons of torque but lacking in the Hp department.  You can afford to lose a few lb. ft. of torque and gain 5 or so Hp.||||reg||1
Z||000007||sjp777||05-11-2001||12:28 AM||||I've had it both ways, with just a straight pipe and with the cherry bomb. Sound is way better with the CB, and engine runs smoother. Don't knock it if you haven't tried it<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||05-11-2001||12:40 AM||||Will the CB be really loud? I am going for loud also, but a nice sounding loud. I will have to pick one up at Advance Auto tomarrow. How hard, how long to install? My converter is not welded on, just clamped. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000009||MAT88GT||05-11-2001||12:46 AM||||If the cat is clamped on you might have some problems getting it off...often when you clamp one exhaust pipe over another it dimples the inside pipe making it difficult to remove the cat. My car is pretty loud for a v6, I have a wav. of it somewhere. I'll put a link up if I can find it.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Mustang Gt 351w/c4 trans, custom 6pt <BR>1985 Fiero Gt some mods<BR>1986 Fiero 2m4 mint condition||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||05-11-2001||12:49 AM||||Actually a shop had it off less then a year ago, to check for build-up and a clogged converter. So it should be pretty easy to take off, I hope.||||reg||1
Z||000011||sjp777||05-11-2001||01:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Will the CB be really loud? I am going for loud also, but a nice sounding loud. I will have to pick one up at Advance Auto tomarrow. How hard, how long to install? My converter is not welded on, just clamped. Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>If it is already clamped on, you have it made. You should be able to just remove and replace. Keep the cat for emissions tests. I usually drop the whole exhaust when I have to put the cat back on, but even that takes less than an hour start to finish. The CB is definitely louder than the cat, but not as loud as the straight pipe. And yes, it is a good loud<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Nashco||05-11-2001||01:23 AM||||I agree that I would never put a cherry bomb on my car to save my life.  The number one reason is that they are just fiberglass packed, and especially with the kind of heat you're subjecting it to in our cars in that location, it will burn out very quickly.  Second is that they are not very rust resistant.  I know they don't often rust through (they wouldn't last that long anyway), but they do get lots of surface rust that you won't get with stainless pipe or a converter.  Third is that I don't support the removal of the cat con, especially on cars like ours that have hardly any restriction as a result.<P>I really don't understand why people remove the cat.  I do understand that there is a certain noise achieved, but why is it that the noise of a pipe without a converter is a good noise?  With today's free flowing cats available, why not just put one of those in?  If there's no difference notice, why don't we just save some of the environment in exchange for "the noise" that you want.  Not to mention, this is law abiding, as minimally important as that may seem to most of you.  If you're running so much power that a free flow cat is restrictive, upgrade to a larger diameter.<P>Well, just my thoughts.  I'm a strong supporter of clean power.  There are cars built today that have super low emissions, and still a lot of power thanks to good engine management.  There are also cars that have been updated with modern cats and engine controls that have actually gained power and emissions prowess as a result.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||Spektyr||05-11-2001||06:38 AM||||Well while I do have to admit that my car sports a straight pipe instead of a cat, and I really don't care about what kind of emissions come out the tailpipe, there is one reason I didn't even consider a free-flow cat.<P>Dollars.<P>Straight pipe is CHEAP.  High Performace Cats are not.<P>Yes, my car sounds vaguely (and I do mean vaguely) ricey at high RPM with no load (revving it), but it sounds great when accelerating and decelerating in gear.  (Gear braking sounds awesome).  I like it this way and just as importantly, I can afford it this way.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000014||JasonS||05-11-2001||07:27 AM||||I'll agree, my '84 sounds much better and runs 100% better with the cat off.  I wasn't about to spend money on a new cat when I can purchase a $8 exhaust repair kit to put in its place.<P>Jason||||reg||14
Z||000015||Seanh||05-11-2001||07:34 AM||||Yeah, cheapness, that my concern exactly. Considering I currently go thru 1.5 tanks of gas every 2 days, I'm filling up every other day, and if something as simle at this could squeeze out a little extra gas milage then I'm all for it. I know the gain probally won't be much, but it should at least be somewhat better. I think I'v decided to go with a straight pipe. So, what should I get at the parts store today? Probally a 22" long 2 1/4" dia exhaust pipe? Then just un clamp, remove the cat and replace it with pipe, reclamp and go driving? I'm still also using a stock muffler, so I hope this change doesn't kill, or do I? Perhaps a new Flowmaster would be in my future?!?!||||reg||1
Z||000016||JasonS||05-11-2001||09:09 AM||||My cat was welded to the exhaust, so I just purchased an exhaust repair kit, sawzall'd the cat off, and clamped the new pipe in its place.  Worked great and didn't take a lot of time to do.<P>Jason||||reg||3
